{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3244","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3244","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3244\",\"parent_revision\":\"32af26047aed0cff8fa7bf78d7e88848c8496de0\",\"root_revision\":\"9eb1f6bcbedece679af97aa170e63afaf253675f05e2d89f28aa863cb7deb0d1\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-27\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nprocess-siren (plugin-creator:process-siren agent / process-siren:woo-sailor skill) produces Mermaid diagrams inline in the target file under a fidelity mandate \u2014 one node per prose step/condition/outcome, nothing merged or omitted. When the target is a skill's SKILL.md, a large diagram can push the file's token count over skilllint's SK006/AS005 threshold (4400 tokens), triggering a warning that must then be resolved by hand.\\n\\nObserved: running woo-sailor's full-step-fidelity rebuild against plugins/development-harness/skills/multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md's Dispatch Flow diagram (PR #3243) pushed the file from 4306 to 4977 tokens, over the threshold. A separate follow-up agent then had to manually relocate the diagram to a references/dispatch-flow.md file behind a context pointer to clear the warning \u2014 this repo's existing progressive-disclosure convention (mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents) already supports this pattern, and references/acceptance-test-guide.md in the same skill is a working precedent.\\n\\nprocess-siren should make this decision itself instead of requiring a manual follow-up every time: when a diagram it produces is reference/verification material that some execution paths don't need (information-hierarchy tier 3), and inlining it would push the target file over the token-budget threshold, it should extract the diagram to a disclosed reference file reached by a context pointer, rather than always inlining. When a diagram is load-bearing content every execution path needs (information-hierarchy tier 1), it should stay inline regardless of size \u2014 a blanket always-extract-when-large rule would incorrectly push genuinely load-bearing diagrams behind a pointer that every invocation needs to see immediately.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: Tie the extraction threshold to skilllint's own 4400-token SK006/AS005 line (or a diagram-specific line count). Update the plugin-creator:process-siren agent definition and/or the process-siren:woo-sailor skill's dispatch logic to make this decision automatically. The extracted file should follow the existing references/*.md + context-pointer pattern already used elsewhere in this repo, not a bare .mmd/.mermaid file.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T01:36:09.699754Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: process-siren should auto-disclose large Mermaid diagrams to references/ instead of always inlining\\nDate: 2026-08-27\\nGoal: Change process-siren/woo-sailor's diagram-authoring behavior so it never leaves duplicated diagram+prose sequencing in a target file, and discloses genuinely reference-only diagrams to references/ when inlining would exceed the file's token budget.\\nConditions:\\n1. What agent file(s) currently define process-siren's diagram-authoring behavior, and does that definition already contain any prose-duplication-avoidance or placement logic? | Status: DERIVABLE (read plugin-creator:process-siren agent definition and process-siren:woo-sailor skill directly \u2014 not yet read this session, only their tool-listing descriptions are known)\\n2. Is skilllint's 4400-token SK006/AS005 threshold queryable/importable by process-siren, or is it a constant that would need to be duplicated/hardcoded into the agent's own logic? | Status: DERIVABLE (grep skilllint source/config for the threshold definition and any exposed API)\\n3. Does this repo have any existing prose/diagram-duplication detection tooling (skilllint checks, markdownlint rules, or similar) that could be reused rather than building new detection logic? | Status: DERIVABLE (search skilllint and markdownlint-cli2 rule sets)\\n4. Is the \\\"load-bearing for every execution path\\\" vs \\\"reference/verification material\\\" distinction mechanically decidable by an LLM agent from the target file's structure alone (e.g., \\\"referenced from more than one Step\\\" vs \\\"referenced from a single cross-check section\\\"), or does it require judgment that resists a hard rule? | Status: AVAILABLE (established this session \u2014 a diagram every execution path needs stays inline; the working example is dh:work-backlog-item's routing sections, which are consulted on every invocation of that route)\\n5. Should retrofit detection (finding and resolving duplication already present in files process-siren did not just edit) be in scope for this item, versus scoping process-siren to only avoid introducing new duplication going forward? | Status: AVAILABLE (Anti-Goal 1 already scopes retrofit out \u2014 the one identified concrete retrofit instance is tracked separately as #3246)\\nAVAILABLE count: 2\\nDERIVABLE count: 3\\nMISSING count: 0\\n</div>\\n\\n## Research\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T01:40:11.189168Z</sub>\\n\\n### Wave 0 research \u2014 three DERIVABLE conditions (2026-08-27)\\n\\n**1. Current diagram-placement / duplication behavior in process-siren and woo-sailor**\\n\\nSource: `plugins/process-siren/agents/process-siren.md` (full file read), `plugins/process-siren/skills/woo-sailor/SKILL.md` (full file read), `plugins/process-siren/skills/improve-processes/SKILL.md` (full file read).\\n\\n- `process-siren.md` Step 6 (\\\"Replace or Return\\\") is binary and unconditional: \\\"If operating on a file: replace the original content with the diagram using Edit\\\" / \\\"If called standalone: return the Mermaid source in a fenced code block.\\\" There is no size check, no token-count check, and no branch that ever writes the diagram to a separate `references/*.md` file. Every diagram the agent produces is inlined into the target file (or returned inline to the caller) regardless of how large it is.\\n- Step 7 (\\\"Annotate the Replacement\\\") only adds a one-time `[!IMPORTANT]` authoritative-procedure notice plus a one-sentence procedure label immediately above each diagram \u2014 no logic here either for extraction or for detecting duplicate prose.\\n- There is no duplication-avoidance logic anywhere in the agent file. Step 6 only ever replaces \\\"the original content\\\" of the section being converted (i.e., it consumes the prose it's converting), but the agent has no step that scans the rest of the document for other prose elsewhere that describes the same process being diagrammed \u2014 that case is entirely unhandled. The word \\\"extract\\\" appears only twice in the file, both scoped to node-level graph refactoring (\\\"extract that description into a separate annotation node\\\" \u2014 i.e., splitting a Mermaid node label into a child annotation node connected by `-.->`), not file-level extraction of a diagram to `references/`.\\n- `woo-sailor/SKILL.md` is a pure dispatch/argument-routing skill (glob path, dry-run vs live, single-file vs directory, spawn `process-siren:process-siren` per file) \u2014 it contains zero diagram-placement or duplication logic; it only decides which files to spawn the agent against and relays BLOCKED verdicts.\\n- `improve-processes/SKILL.md` (loaded by process-siren before conversion) governs process *quality* (Excellence Checklist, Pre-Conversion Completeness Gate, Triage Protocol) \u2014 it validates whether the source is well-structured enough to convert, not where the resulting diagram is placed or whether it duplicates other prose.\\n\\nConclusion: process-siren today only produces/validates the diagram itself (via the MCP `validate_and_render_mermaid_diagram` / `get_diagram_summary` tools referenced in Step 4) and always inlines it. It has neither a disclosure mechanism nor any duplication-avoidance logic for pre-existing prose describing the same process.\\n\\n**2. Is the skilllint SK006/AS005 4400-token threshold available programmatically?**\\n\\nSource: local skilllint checkout at `/Users/jamienelson/repos/skilllint` (`packages/skilllint/limits.py`, `rules/as_series.py`, `rules/sk_series.py`), plus live CLI checks (`uvx skilllint@latest check --help`, `uvx skilllint@latest rule AS005`, `uvx skilllint@latest rule SK006`, `uvx skilllint@latest rules --help`).\\n\\n- Yes \u2014 as a Python constant, not as a CLI flag. `packages/skilllint/limits.py` defines `BODY_TOKEN_WARNING: int = 4400` (aliased `TOKEN_WARNING_THRESHOLD`) and `BODY_TOKEN_ERROR: int = 8800` (aliased `TOKEN_ERROR_THRESHOLD`), explicitly documented as \\\"the single source of truth for all limits, thresholds, and constraints enforced by skilllint,\\\" sourced from the agentskills.io progressive-disclosure spec (`< 5000 tokens recommended`, 4400 chosen as warning headroom).\\n- Verified importable at runtime without adding skilllint as a project dependency: `uvx --from skilllint python -c \\\"from skilllint.limits import BODY_TOKEN_WARNING, BODY_TOKEN_ERROR; print(BODY_TOKEN_WARNING, BODY_TOKEN_ERROR)\\\"` \u2192 printed `4400 8800`. Since process-siren's agent frontmatter already grants it the `Bash` tool, it can query the live value this way instead of hardcoding `4400`.\\n- The CLI itself does **not** expose the threshold as structured/parseable output: `check --help` has `--tokens-only` (prints the *actual* token count of a file, not the threshold) but no `--threshold`/`--json` flag; `rule AS005` and `rule SK006` print the number only embedded in formatted prose (\\\"Warning at 4400 tokens \u2014 consider splitting\\\"), which is a docs-rendering path, not a stable machine-readable contract. `rules --help` offers only `--platform`/`--category`/`--severity` filters, no JSON output mode.\\n- Recommendation for the eventual fix: import the constant via the `uvx --from skilllint python -c ...` one-liner (or equivalent) rather than hardcoding `4400` in process-siren's own logic \u2014 the CLI's prose-embedded number is not a stable contract to parse, but the Python constant is the documented \\\"single source of truth.\\\"\\n\\n**3. Does this repo already have tooling that detects \\\"prose and diagram describe the same content\\\" duplication?**\\n\\nSource: skilllint rule catalog (`uvx skilllint@latest rules`, grep of `duplicat` across `/Users/jamienelson/repos/skilllint/packages/skilllint/rules/*.py`), this repo's `.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc`, and a repo-wide grep for SK006/AS005 usage outside skilllint itself.\\n\\n- No such tooling exists. `uvx skilllint@latest rules` has no rule whose name or description mentions duplication/redundancy/overlap. Every \\\"duplicat\\\" hit inside skilllint's own rule source (`hk_series.py`, `lk_series.py`, `nr_series.py`, `pl_series.py`, `pr_series.py`, `pd_series.py`, `sl_series.py`, `tc_series.py`) concerns duplicate *identifiers* (duplicate hook registrations, duplicate skill/plugin names, duplicate frontmatter keys) \u2014 none of it is semantic content-duplication detection between a prose passage and a diagram.\\n- This repo's `.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc` explicitly disables `MD024` (duplicate headings) and has no content-similarity rule; markdownlint operates on markdown structure, not cross-block semantic overlap, so it could not detect \\\"this paragraph says the same thing as that diagram\\\" even in principle.\\n- Conclusion: any duplication-avoidance logic for #3244's fix (detecting that pre-existing prose already describes the process now being diagrammed, and removing/collapsing it) would need to be built from scratch inside process-siren's own workflow \u2014 there is no existing repo rule or skilllint rule to reuse or extend for this.\\n\\nSTATUS: DONE \u2014 all three angles returned concrete answers (no gaps); see citations above.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 surfaced while running the multi-perspective-review skill-tightening pipeline (PRs #3240-#3243)\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-27\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Note\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T00:58:02.950912Z</sub>\\n\\nprocess-siren's purpose is a process clarifier and communicator for the reading agent, not a diagram-drawing agent \u2014 it already does the harder work first (analyzes the whole picture before drawing, follows workflow/graph design methodology when it does draw, then validates Mermaid syntax and layout). That framing should drive the inline-vs-disclose decision this item asks for: the trigger is not simply \\\"the diagram is large\\\" or \\\"the file crossed a token threshold\\\" \u2014 it is whether keeping the diagram inline still serves process clarity for the agent that has to follow it, versus whether disclosure would serve clarity better (or at least not harm it) while relieving token pressure. A load-bearing diagram every execution path needs stays inline even if large, because inlining IS what serves clarity there; a diagram that is reference/verification material only some paths consult is a stronger disclosure candidate on clarity grounds alone, and the token threshold is corroborating evidence, not the primary trigger.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T01:03:45.940092Z</sub>\\n\\nSharper correction (supersedes the \\\"inline vs disclose\\\" framing above as the primary lens): the real failure mode this session's passes (#3240, #3241, #3243, #3245 against multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md) never fixed is prose/diagram duplication, not diagram placement. Agents consume Mermaid as notation, not as a rendered picture \u2014 they never \\\"see\\\" the diagram, they parse the text. That means the diagram's value is notation density and branching precision, not visual communication. The anti-pattern is keeping a prose walkthrough of the same sequencing/branching the diagram already encodes \u2014 that is the same \\\"duplication \u2014 same meaning in more than one place\\\" failure mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents already names, and it inflates the file's token footprint independent of whether the diagram itself is inline or disclosed to references/.\\n\\nThe corrected design: the diagram should be the sole authoritative representation of sequencing and branching logic. Prose should carry only what the notation cannot \u2014 exact MCP call signatures, payload/field shapes, schema pointers \u2014 not a second narration of the same branches. This repo already has a working precedent for this shape: dh:work-backlog-item's routing sections contain only a Mermaid diagram with zero parallel prose re-describing the same branches.\\n\\nConcrete follow-up (separate, more foundational than the disclosure heuristic above): multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md's Steps 1-7 prose still fully restates the same branching the (now-disclosed) Dispatch Flow diagram encodes. Converging these to one representation would likely eliminate most of the token pressure that motivated the disclosure work in the first place \u2014 the auto-disclosure heuristic above becomes a secondary concern once the duplication itself is removed, since a de-duplicated skill is naturally smaller.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"f7f2a2668f29c7f8901c61c3c3fed103d4c8222d358124e010c236feaeff08c4\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQ9kjUQ\"}"}